\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $n\ge2$ and let $\gamma\subset\mathbb C^n$ be a Jordan arc, the image of a continuous injective map from $[0,1]$. Let $\pi_j:\mathbb C^n\to\mathbb C$ be the $j$th coordinate projection, and assume that each $\pi_j(\gamma)$ has two-dimensional Lebesgue measure zero. Let $C(\gamma)$ have its uniform norm. Write $P(\gamma)$ for the uniform closure of restrictions of complex polynomials in $n$ variables, and $R(\gamma)$ for the uniform closure of restrictions of rational functions with no poles on $\gamma$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Must $P(\gamma)=C(\gamma)$? The source records $R(\gamma)=C(\gamma)$ under the same projection hypotheses. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does polynomial approximation of all continuous functions hold on every higher-dimensional Jordan arc whose coordinate projections have area zero?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2309008 (AMR-022-9008). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory} (2018), arXiv:1809.07200. Problem 9.8, complete original question, all following updates, and the relevant chapter definitions. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
